SCHEDULED SLA REPORT EMAIL NOTICES
================================

Scope
-----
Scheduled reports generate PDF archives. On-demand generation supports PDF/CSV.
Email sends a generic report-ready notice with an authenticated link to /sla/reports.
No attachments, report contents, resource names, credentials or public download
tokens are sent. SMTP and Gmail SMTP are supported; API-based mail providers,
arbitrary external recipients, bounce processing and inbox-delivery confirmation
are outside this implementation. The Reports tab currently uses English labels.

Upgrade and opt-in
-----------------
Back up the database and report volume using the coordinated deployment procedure.
Migration 017 adds a durable outbox and leaves delivery_enabled=false on existing
schedules. Existing recipient metadata does not authorize any email. Migration
017 resets SMTP provider verification because older STARTTLS verification did
not explicitly validate server certificates. Existing alert sending remains
independent of the report delivery opt-in.

Migration 018 adds a per-notice deployment identifier (delivery_epoch). Existing
notices remain unapproved with a NULL identifier; it is never backfilled from
configuration. Existing version-1 schedule approvals need review and reapproval.
Old unclaimed notices become unknown, not retried, even if attempts is zero.

1. Configure FRONTEND_URL as the public HTTPS origin, without credentials, path,
   query or fragment. API and worker services must receive the same value.
   Set SLA_REPORT_DELIVERY_EPOCH to a newly generated UUID4, for example with
   python -c "import uuid; print(uuid.uuid4())". Keep this value identical across
   API, scheduler and every worker, stable across ordinary restarts. Empty disables
   mail approval and quarantines active notices. An invalid value refuses startup.
   It is a deployment identifier, not a secret or vault key. Manage it separately
   from database/report snapshots, and rotate it before restoring workers.
2. Use an enabled SMTP or Gmail SMTP provider belonging to the organization.
   Require STARTTLS. Verify it again after upgrade. The OS trust store is used;
   mount a managed CA bundle through SSL_CERT_FILE when a private CA is required.
   Never disable verification to make a private certificate work.
3. In SLA > Reports, review the schedule's calendar and explicit policies.
   Site-limited operators must select policy IDs. Scheduling runs hourly at :07;
   the stored local calendar time is not a minute-level delivery guarantee.
4. Opt into notices and select the verified provider. Recipients must be active
   FreeSDN users in the same organization with config:read and report access.
   Save and reapprove. Only future scheduled reports enqueue notices.
5. Run Celery beat and a worker consuming the metrics queue. Delivery polling
   runs every minute, at most eight notices per invocation. Pending work survives
   lost broker ticks in PostgreSQL and is picked up by a later polling pass.

Authority and cancellation
--------------------------
Approvals pin the actor's permissions/site ceiling, recipient identities and
addresses/site ceilings, provider configuration digest, public origin and delivery epoch.
Each attempt checks current actor and recipient authority, original report site
scope, enabled schedule/provider, unchanged approval and readable archive file.
Removing the last site grant cannot make unattended mail unrestricted.
Provider changes, account/email/scope changes and schedule edits cancel affected
pending notices. Edit/save to approve future reports; old notices are not redirected.
Pausing is possible even when the provider configuration is broken.

The claim is committed before network I/O. Changes after that claim may be too
late to stop a send already in progress. Only pending/retry deliveries can be
cancelled through the UI/API. Authentication and site checks still apply when
the recipient follows the link. No claim of atomic revocation during SMTP I/O
or exactly-once external delivery is made.

States and recovery
-------------------
pending/retry: awaiting an attempt; UI shows the next attempt and can cancel it.
sending: a durable claim exists; concurrent workers skip it.
accepted: the SMTP server acknowledged DATA; this is not proof of inbox delivery.
failed: definite permanent refusal, exhausted retries, expired notice or corrupt
        configuration. Fix the cause and review the next scheduled report.
cancelled: approval/identity/provider/report is no longer valid, or an operator
           cancelled unclaimed work. No automatic resend follows reapproval.
skipped: recipient email preferences or the reports category suppressed notice.
unknown: acknowledgement was lost, the overall deadline expired, a sending
         worker disappeared, or the notice belongs to an unapproved recovery epoch. NEVER automatically resend. Inspect SMTP relay logs
         using Message-ID <sla-report-DELIVERY_UUID@freesdn.invalid> and ensure
         the original worker has stopped before any external manual action.
         No in-app resend or forced retry of unknown outcomes is provided.

Only explicit transient refusals and pre-submission connection failures retry.
There are at most five SMTP attempts, with 15/30/60/120 minute delays. Socket I/O
has a 10-second timeout and the async send deadline is 35 seconds. An interrupted
claim becomes unknown after ten minutes; a surviving SMTP thread may still finish
after the async deadline. A failed DB commit after acceptance also leaves a claim
that becomes unknown, not retryable. Paused/expired/corrupt notices do not block
the rest of the outbox. Notices expire after seven days. Quiet hours defer them.
Provider hourly/daily counters are shared with notifications; unlike urgent alert
sending, report notices defer when rate-limit storage is unavailable.

Operator APIs (organization and archive site permissions enforced)
-----------------------------------------------------------------
GET /api/v1/sla/reports/{report_id}/deliveries (requires config:write)
POST /api/v1/sla/reports/{report_id}/deliveries/{delivery_id}/cancel
POST /api/v1/sla/report-schedules; PUT /api/v1/sla/report-schedules/{id}
  delivery_enabled: boolean (default false)
  delivery_provider_id: organization SMTP provider UUID
  recipients: approved organization user email addresses

History, backup and rollback
----------------------------
Preserve the database, encrypted provider configuration and report volume together.
The outbox stores recipient addresses (personal data), fixed error codes and
attempt counts, never SMTP credentials or report bodies. Terminal delivery history
has no automatic deletion policy in this version: size/retention review is required.
Migration downgrade refuses to discard nonempty delivery history. Preserve and
review that history rather than clearing it to make rollback pass.

Coordinated report-mail recovery
-------------------------------
A restored database can contain pending/retry/sending states for mail already
accepted after the backup. Even attempts=0 is not proof that no email was sent.
The epoch is NOT automatic restore detection: restoring the old configuration
with the old database can replay mail. SMTP Message-ID does not deduplicate it.

1. Fence the original API, schedulers and workers, including their SMTP/network
   egress. Preserve the source database, report volume, deployment configuration,
   relay logs and all backups. A changed setting cannot fence an old running
   process or stop an already-submitted message. Do not use a rolling restart.
2. Restore into an isolated environment. Complete the broader database, JWT,
   vault, queue/HA and source-election recovery checks before restarting general
   workers. This report procedure does not authorize hardware-job replay.
3. Generate a NEW UUID4 for SLA_REPORT_DELIVERY_EPOCH outside the restored backup.
   Do not reuse the original value, generate a new value on every boot, copy it
   from restored approvals or modify notice epochs in the database. Keep the
   credential encryption SECRET_KEY and ENCRYPTION_SALT unchanged. JWT recovery
   requires its separate signing-key cutover; this identifier does not replace it.
4. Apply the current migrations with workers stopped. Recreate all API, beat and
   worker processes with the same new epoch only when the coordinated recovery
   is accepted. Mixed epoch values can quarantine each other's notices.
5. Delivery polling quarantines old active notices as unknown with the fixed code
   delivery_recovery_review_required, in batches of at most 100 per process_one
   call, including future retries and unexpired sending claims. It preserves IDs,
   recipient snapshots, attempts and all terminal history. No SMTP is attempted
   for these rows. Pending current-epoch work can proceed alongside quarantine.
   Due schedules with stale approvals pause before rendering a report.
6. Review the original source and relay logs against each notice Message-ID.
   Missing relay history is not proof of nondelivery. Unknown notices have no
   automatic or UI resend action. Do not clear history to make a downgrade work.
7. Once the restored authority/provider/archive state has been reviewed, edit and
   save the intended schedules in SLA > Reports. Only newly generated reports can
   create notices under the new approval. Reapproval cannot revive old notices.
8. Keep the current recovery-aware image for this database. Migration 018 refuses
   downgrade with nonempty outbox history. An older image can bypass the epoch
   guard even without a database downgrade; pin approved images and prohibit that
   rollback. Recovery after new writes needs reconciliation, not snapshot swapping.

Local rehearsal (synthetic, no customer relay or hardware)
--------------------------------------------------------
python scripts/check_report_recovery.py --backend CANDIDATE_BACKEND_IMAGE \
  --postgres RETAINED_POSTGRES_IMAGE --valkey RETAINED_VALKEY_IMAGE \
  --work-dir /absolute/new/private/report-recovery

The harness resolves exact image IDs, creates a private internal Docker network,
uses real PostgreSQL dump/restore and a real loopback SMTP STARTTLS peer, and removes
only its own containers/anonymous volumes/network. It reproduces duplicate delivery
with an unchanged epoch, then checks rotated/empty epochs, reapproval and restart.
The private directory contains synthetic credentials and snapshots: never publish
it. Only report.json is intended as shareable evidence. No existing data is mounted.

These tests do not qualify a customer's relay, DNS/SPF/DKIM/DMARC, deliverability,
volume capacity, representative backup, source fencing or coordinated HA recovery.
